Draft: the skeleton of the article, on the template every article in
this group follows. `tools/articles.sh` skips it until the `draft` line
leaves the front matter. AFT is the article; tobit and interval
censoring are a paragraph and a panel each, as the same latent-Gaussian
machinery. BART joins Suggests with this article.

## Introduction

The lognormal accelerated failure time model (Wei 1992), ln T_i =
f(x_i) + e_i, fitted by censored-data augmentation: each right-censored
row's latent log time is refreshed from N(f(x_i), sigma^2) truncated to
[ln t_i, inf) before the sweep, and the completed response runs the
Gaussian sweep unchanged. The model of the BART package's `abart`.
Tobit uses the same refresh at fixed limits; interval censoring
refreshes into [ln l_i, ln u_i]. Reach for it when times are
right-censored: a Gaussian fit to observed times treats censored rows
as events and is biased downward.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The AFT likelihood with the censored contribution and the refresh, from
`docs/models.md`, "AFT survival", "Tobit" and "Interval-censored". Link
to `crates/thiessen/src/models/aft.rs`, `tobit.rs`,
`interval_censored.rs` and `censoring.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
library(survival)
data <- lognormal_friedman(n = 300, p = 5, seed = 1)
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule)
aft <- paired_fits(data, list(aft = control), seeds)$aft
summary(aft[[1]])
```

The response selects the family: `Surv(time, event)` fits AFT under
the default control. A Gaussian fit to the observed log times, with the
censored rows at their censoring values, is the default comparator;
`paired_fits(response = )` arrives with this article to fit both on one
data set. `benchmarks/upstream/aft_abart.R` uses n = 200; this page
uses n = 300. Held out: 500 uncensored rows with their true log times.
External comparators, one fit per seed: `BART::abart` at matched size
and `survival::survreg(dist = "lognormal")`, the parametric baseline the
field starts from.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: predicted against true log time on the held-out
rows, Gaussian and AFT, coloured by whether the nearest training row
was censored.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: survival-probability calibration at three fixed
t, nominal against empirical, AFT, `abart` and `survreg`, beside the
table (RMSE on log time, coverage and width of the log-time interval,
coverage of S(t | x) from the predictive draws).

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: bias of the posterior mean log time against the
censored share in {0, 1/3, 2/3}; AFT holds and the Gaussian fit drifts;
at zero censoring the two are the same chain draw for draw.

```{r boundary}
```

Figure 4, siblings, two panels with a two-sentence paragraph each:
tobit, a one-covariate surface censored below with about 30 per cent of
rows at the limit, fit against truth with the limit drawn; interval
censoring, the surface observed through inspection bins as vertical
segments with both fits through them.

```{r siblings}
```

## Validity

Stem `aft` shown; `tobit` and `interval_censored` named with a link.
Mention the known-answer tests (censored-likelihood quadrature) and
that all-event data reproduces the Gaussian model on ln t draw for
draw.

```{r battery, results = "asis"}
cat(readLines("figures/censoring/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

